      <h2>1. Understanding Loneliness in the Digital Age</h2>

      <p>Loneliness is the unpleasant feeling that can occur when a person's desired level of social connection is greater than the connection they feel they currently have. It is different from simply being physically alone.</p>

      <p>The World Health Organization estimates that around one in six people worldwide experience loneliness. The issue affects people across age groups and regions, although some groups experience higher rates than others.</p>

      <h3>1.1 Being Alone Is Not the Same as Being Lonely</h3>

      <p>Being alone describes a person's physical or social circumstances. Loneliness describes how connected that person feels.</p>

      <p>Someone may enjoy spending time alone and not feel lonely. Conversely, someone may have many online contacts, classmates, colleagues, or followers and still feel disconnected.</p>

      <h3>1.2 Loneliness and Social Isolation Are Different</h3>

      <p>Social isolation generally describes having limited social relationships or contact, while loneliness describes a subjective feeling of disconnection. The two can occur together, but they are not identical.</p>

      <h3>1.3 Why Loneliness Matters</h3>

      <p>Research has associated persistent loneliness and social isolation with a range of physical and mental health risks. The CDC lists associations with depression, anxiety, cardiovascular disease, stroke, dementia and other health outcomes.</p>

      <p>This does not mean that every lonely person will develop a health condition. It means that persistent social disconnection is an important factor in overall health and wellbeing.</p>

      <h2>Loneliness vs Depression: What's the Difference?</h2>

      <p>Loneliness and depression can occur together, but they are not the same experience. Someone experiencing loneliness may primarily want more meaningful social connection, while depression is a mental health condition involving a broader pattern of symptoms.</p>

