      <h2>2. Why Human Connection Matters</h2>

      <p>People have social needs as well as physical needs. Supportive relationships can provide companionship, emotional support, a sense of belonging, and practical help during difficult periods.</p>

      <h3>2.1 Why Conversation Can Matter</h3>

      <p>Conversation gives people an opportunity to exchange experiences, ask questions, listen, and feel acknowledged by another person.</p>

      <p>Even short interactions can sometimes feel meaningful. Research discussed by the American Psychological Association has found that people often underestimate how enjoyable conversations with strangers can be.</p>

      <h3>2.2 Quality Matters More Than Simply Being Connected</h3>

      <p>Having many contacts does not automatically mean a person feels socially connected. The quality, reliability and meaning of relationships matter as well.</p>

      <h3>2.3 Online Connection Can Be One Part of a Social Life</h3>

      <p>Online communication can complement relationships with friends, family, colleagues, classmates and local communities. For some people, it can make social interaction easier when distance, schedules, mobility or other circumstances make face-to-face interaction difficult.</p>

